[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 475,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 432,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67703,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 432,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Desmoid fibromatosis is a locally aggressive but non-metastasizing (myo)fibroblastic neoplasm with infiltrative growth and propensity for local recurrence.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 476,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 433,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67704,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 433,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8821/1 Desmoid-type fibromatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 477,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 434,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67705,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 434,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F7C &amp; XH13Z3 Neoplasms of uncertain behaviour of connective or other soft tissue &amp; Desmoid-type fibromatosis (aggressive fibromatosis)</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 478,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 435,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67706,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 435,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> aggressive fibromatosis; desmoid tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 479,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 436,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67707,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 436,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 480,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 437,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67708,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 437,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The majority of intrathoracic desmoid fibromatoses arise from the chest wall; however, cases originating from the pleura, pulmonary parenchyma, and mediastinum have been reported {679743; 17090192; 28031839; 9950156; 3824278; 26774632; 26717381; 25295113; 9930504; 8188925}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 481,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 438,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67709,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 438,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Intrathoracic desmoid fibromatosis may cause pain, dyspnoea, and kyphoscoliosis or may be detected as an incidental radiological finding {679743; 11881738}. Cases after trauma/surgery have also been reported {20139256; 23558226}.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>The imaging findings are non-distinct, but MRI often shows mixed hyperintense and isointense signals {9265665; 3823958}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 482,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 439,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67710,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 439,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Intrathoracic desmoids most often arise in adulthood and show an equal sex distribution {679743; 11881738}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 483,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 440,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67711,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 440,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology of desmoid fibromatosis includes genetic factors, and (as at other sites) some cases may be associated with prior trauma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 484,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 441,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67712,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 441,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The majority of cases (as many as 95%) are sporadic, resulting from somatic mutations in <em>CTNNB1</em>; a smaller subset occur in patients harbouring germline <em>APC</em> mutations (Gardner syndrome) {20197769; 26171757; 11241320; 8150351}. Both genetic events lead to increased activation of the WNT/&beta;-catenin pathway {19099242; 28575679; 17952864}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 491,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 448,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67713,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 448,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gross examination reveals a poorly circumscribed solid mass with a firm, white-tan whorled or trabecular cut surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 485,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 442,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67714,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 442,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Histological examination reveals long sweeping fascicles of fibroblasts and myofibroblasts, without cytological atypia, that infiltrate into surrounding soft tissue. Lymphoid aggregates are frequently appreciated at the periphery of the lesion. Mitoses may be seen, but atypical mitotic figures are absent. A subset of cases may show myxoid change or keloidal-type collagen deposition. The lesional cells express SMA and MSA in a tram-track pattern, with variable desmin staining. Nuclear &beta;-catenin expression is present in approximately 80% of cases {17711447}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 486,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 443,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67715,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 443,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>FNA specimens may contain fibroblasts/myofibroblasts without atypia or nuclear hyperchromasia; however, the nonspecific cytological features make diagnosis by FNA challenging {8498134}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 487,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 444,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67716,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 444,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>CTNNB1</em> mutation studies may be used to confirm the diagnosis on limited biopsy specimens in the absence of diagnostic morphological features and equivocal &beta;-catenin immunostaining {21884214; 22766794}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 490,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 447,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67717,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 447,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Long fascicles of (myo)fibroblasts lacking atypia with an infiltrative growth pattern</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em></p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Nuclear &beta;-catenin expression by immunohistochemistry</li>\r\n<li><em>CTNNB1</em> mutation studies may be useful to confirm the diagnosis on small specimens</li>\r\n</ul>",
        "timestamp": "2021-02-23T18:10:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 488,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 445,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67718,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 445,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 489,
    "bookid": 35,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 446,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 67719,
        "bookId": 35,
        "chapterId": 182,
        "headingId": 446,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The clinical behaviour of desmoid fibromatosis is unpredictable, with recurrence rates as high as 33%. However, margin status does not consistently correlate with recurrence risk, and an initial watchful waiting approach for asymptomatic patients has been advocated {28961825}. <em>CTNNB1</em> p.S45F mutations may correlate with increased recurrence risk {21323417; 23913621; 20197769; 18832571}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-12-03T16:49:13"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]